Refactoring variable names to use snake_case means changing the naming style of variables in a codebase to use all lowercase letters, with spaces between words replaced by underscores (). It is a code cleaning process (refactoring) designed to improve readability, ensure consistency, and adhere to specific language standards, most notably in Python. 
Key Aspects of the Request 

• Lowercase Only: All letters are in lowercase. 
• Underscore Separation: Words are separated by an underscore. 
• Refactoring: This implies changing existing names or to the new standard throughout the entire project. 

Why Use ? 

1. Readability: The underscores act as visual separators, making it easier for humans to scan and distinguish words compared to long  strings. 
2. Language Standards:  is the official naming convention for variables, functions, and methods in Python (PEP 8), Rust, and Ruby. 
3. Consistency: Applying this style across a project makes the code look uniform and professional. 
4. Database Compatibility: It is highly compatible with database table and column naming conventions. 

